@using Tellurian.Trains.Schedules.Planning.Components.Reporting.Duties

@inject Translator Translator

@{ UseReportCulture(); }

@* The last page of the general instructions booklet: what the layout is, and how the freight entries
   of every other report are written.

   Appended because the people who receive this booklet and no other — station staff above all — get a
   layout overview from nowhere else. The topology and shunting yards are the same ones a duty booklet
   shows, so the component is shared; the section title is not, because the duty booklet's overview
   page carries none.

   The shunting yards table can be taken off this page and placed in the author's own text instead,
   with a <ShuntingYards/> tag. This page carries four things — a section title, a diagram, a table and
   the cargo key — and a layout with more than a handful of shunting yards runs past the foot of it,
   where the page body's overflow:hidden drops whatever did not fit without a word. The table is the
   part that grows with the layout, so it is the part the author can move. *@

<div class="instructions-overview">
    <h1 class="section-title">@Translator("Layout")</h1>
    <DutyOverviewPage Layout="Layout" ShowShuntingYards="ShowShuntingYards" />
    @* The key to the cargo marks and wordings, under a section title of its own: the shunting yards
       above it are layout facts, and this is how the freight entries of every other report are
       written. Both belong on the page the reader keeps coming back to, which is this one. *@
    <CargoFlowExplanation Layout="Layout" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The layout the booklet is printed for.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Layout? Layout { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the shunting yards table is printed here, which it is unless the author placed it in
    /// their own text.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowShuntingYards { get; set; } = true;
}
